// The Spanish half of the names: what each place and its capital are called in
// Spanish, and the other Spanish names people type for them. Written by hand,
// one entry per code in catalog.mjs (build-data.mjs fails if a code is missing
// here, or if this file has one catalog.mjs lacks), so the generated files carry
// no third-party database licence.
//
// Entry shape:  CODE: es(name, aliases, capital)
//   name     the usual Spanish name. The reference is the country list of the
//            RAE's Diccionario panhispánico de dudas (DPD) and Fundéu: the forms
//            in common use across Latin America and Spain (Alemania, Países
//            Bajos, Catar, Baréin, Yibuti, Esuatini, Fiyi, Palaos...).
//   aliases  other Spanish names people type: Spain-vs-Latin-America variants,
//            colloquial and older names, and the international spelling where
//            Spanish texts use it too (Kenya beside Kenia, Rwanda beside Ruanda).
//            The build adds i18n-iso-countries' Spanish names on top and drops
//            anything two places would share, as it does for English.
//   capital  [name, [aliases]] in Spanish, or null exactly where catalog.mjs has
//            no capital. The name repeats the English one where Spanish uses the
//            same (Lima, Ottawa); it is never left out.
//
// Rules the lists follow (also in tools/globle/README.md, "Spanish names"):
//   Each language's list stands on its own: a Spanish alias may be spelled like
//     an English one (Malvinas, Zaire). Spellings that differ only by accents or
//     case are left out, since the matcher folds both (no "Peru" beside Perú).
//   A leading article is not written as a separate alias (Las Bahamas, el Perú,
//     los Estados Unidos): the matcher drops "el", "la", "los" and "las" the way
//     it drops "the". It stays where it is part of the name (El Salvador, La Paz,
//     El Cairo, La Habana).
//   Questionable cases mirror the English data. English lists England, Scotland,
//     Wales and Northern Ireland for the United Kingdom, so Spanish has
//     Inglaterra, Escocia, Gales and Irlanda del Norte. "Bretaña" is left out:
//     in Spanish it is Brittany. "América" is not added for the United States,
//     since to a Spanish speaker it is the continent (the English alias
//     "America" still matches it, accent-folded, as before).
//   Falkland Islands → Islas Malvinas, and its capital → Puerto Argentino
//     (Stanley and Puerto Stanley as aliases): the names Spanish speakers use.
//   Myanmar → Birmania (the DPD's preference; Myanmar is an alias). Timor-Leste
//     → Timor Oriental. Czechia → Chequia (República Checa an alias).
//   An old or rare alias that starts with another answer country's whole name
//     is left out: "Honduras Británica" (Belize), "China Taipéi" (Taiwan). Both
//     languages are always matched, so with them "Honduras" and "China" would
//     be the start of another country's name too, on an English page as well,
//     and Name them all would wait to see whether more was coming. The English
//     forms (British Honduras, Chinese Taipei) still find Belize and Taiwan.
//     Where the clash is the usual Spanish name itself (Corea del Norte, Sudán
//     del Sur, Guinea Ecuatorial) or mirrors the English data (Irlanda del
//     Norte for the United Kingdom) it stays.

const es = (name, aliases = [], capital = null) => ({ name, aliases, capital });

